  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 258 AM EDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nHigh pressure seeps in today in the wake of yesterday's cold front. \nLight northwesterly flow after sunset prompted areas of lake-\nenhanced fog generally along and west of US 31, with patchy \ninstances of dense fog as far east as Huntington and Marion. Overall \nthis fog has been rather transient, with observations bouncing \naround except in a couple pesky locations. Water vapor depicts \na mid- level trough axis now exiting MI which should prompt \nnortheast flow to bring drier air into the region over these \nnext few hours, working to mix out locally dense fog.\n\nFalling shy of a record high temperature by 1 degree at Fort \nWayne yesterday, today will be cooler with highs only in the \nlow-to-mid 60s area-wide (yet normal for this date is 57). \nTemperatures of this magnitude are in store for much of the \nforecast period with the exception of Tuesday and Wednesday when\n70s return thanks to robust warm air advection ahead of a mid \nlatitude cyclone. But first, a weak warm front attempts to lifts\ninto the mid-Missippi valley Saturday morning bringing a 20-30%\nchance of isolated rain showers to areas west of I-69. Plenty \nof dry hours on Halloween but rather cloudy with high \ntemperatures generally near 60.\n\nRain chances become more widespread next week starting with a \ntrough passing through the Ohio valley Sunday afternoon and \nevening (20-30% chance for now, with the trough's track still in\nflux). The primary system we've been monitoring spills onshore \nof the West Coast this weekend, arriving to our doorstep \nTuesday/Wednesday. Low pressure emerges in the Lee of the \nRockies as a deep trough pushes inland. A surface low wraps up \nover the Northern Plains, occludes, prompting renewed \ncyclogenesis of the Central Plains. This spells a period of \nmild and breezy conditions locally on Tuesday and Wednesday as \nthe upper-level pattern becomes highly amplified over the \neastern Great Lakes. A period of steady rain is probable \nWednesday as this system moves through. At this distance, any \nsevere weather risk appears to lie elsewhere owing to a \ndistinct lack of instability. Wet weather may linger into the \nend of the week depending on how the low wraps up over the \neastern CONUS. No significant cool-downs noted in its wake. &&",
